=== Introduction
--- Psalms 1–5 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalms 1–5
sub: The blessed man • Kiss the Son • The Lord my shield • Prayer at night • Prayer in the morning
refs: Psalm 1:1; Psalm 2:12; Psalm 3:3; Psalm 4:8; Psalm 5:3
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse. Press C for the whole chapter.

=== Psalm 1
--- Psalm 1 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 1
sub: The blessed man and the ungodly
refs: Psalm 1:1

--- Songs of the called out assembly
kicker: Psalm 1 · The Psalms
p: The book of Psalms, songs sung by the congregation, musicians and singers, in the called out assembly.
p: Most are attributed to King David, whose playing of the harp soothed his predecessor, King Saul, and while **Psalm 1** isn't clearly introduced as David's, it follows his patterns of praise and doctrine, and generally is believed to be his. But more importantly, they are of the Spirit, like all scripture, and profitable for learning and reproof.
p: And much like the poetic wisdom of Solomon, we find the gospel of Christ throughout.

--- Blessed is the man
kicker: Psalm 1 · The blessed man
big: Psalm 1:1 | mark: Blessed is the man
p: Verse 1 opens with a standard refrain found in **Psalms**; the blessedness of the man, and the understanding that it is by God's grace and mercy all blessings flow, Whose promises are sure.
p: Here, the promise to bless those who do not listen to the ungodly, the wisdom of this world. Blessing those who do not go the way of sinners, or the scorners.
p: In many ways, the Psalms reflect the wisdom of the Lord found in Proverbs, and give an insight to the mind and heart of the King, a man after God's own heart.

--- His delight is in the law of the Lord
kicker: Psalm 1 · Day and night
big: Psalm 1:2 | mark: his delight is in the law of the LORD; in his law doth he meditate day and night
p: David contrasts the ungodly sinners and scorners with the man who delights in the law of God. Who meditates upon the word day and night. This too is a frequent theme found in **Psalms**, and it shows the importance of scripture, and its role in the Christian life.

--- Yea, hath God said?
kicker: Application • The word of God
p: Too often we see those opposed to Christ attacking the inerrant, inspired and preserved word of God. As Satan tempted Eve in the garden, saying "Yea, hath God said?" enemies of God attack His word, because it is the easiest way to cast doubt, or to put false doctrine, vain traditions and damnable heresy in place of the rock which Jesus tells us to build upon; these sayings of His.
p: Even so-called professing Christian sects and denominations, that make their history, mythology and traditions the focal point of their faith will downplay and minimize the importance of scripture, mocking Bible believing Christians for making it the sole authority in our faith and practices.

--- A tree planted by the rivers
kicker: Psalm 1 · The tree
big: Psalm 1:3 | mark: like a tree planted by the rivers of water
p: But what sayeth the scripture? Those that walk in the Spirit, seeking the blessings of God, meditate in His word day and night. By doing so, we become as a tree planted by the river, constantly fed and nourished. Bearing fruit for God. Not withering and being cut off, as the unfruitful. Prospering in all our ways.

--- Like the chaff
kicker: Psalm 1 · The chaff
big: Psalm 1:4 | mark: like the chaff which the wind driveth away
p: The ungodly have no fruit. They are compared to the chaff of wheat, that blows away in the wind on the threshing floor. Insubstantial. Unfruitful. Hollow husks with no purpose other than to be burned up in fire.

--- The congregation of the righteous
kicker: Psalm 1 · The judgment
big: Psalm 1:5 | mark: the congregation of the righteous
p: The distinction between the two types of men, the saved believers, the righteous by faith and the ungodly, who do not walk in faith, is made clear.
p: We will sit in judgment with Christ. We shall be the church of the righteous, the congregation of the elect in Jesus. But the ungodly will not.

--- The way of the ungodly shall perish
kicker: Psalm 1 · The two ways
big: Psalm 1:6
p: Just as Jesus asks Martha after the death of her brother, Lazarus:
big: John 11:26 | mark: Believest thou this?
p: David clearly did. The sinners perish. But the righteous in Christ are with the Lord, always.

=== Psalm 2
--- Psalm 2 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 2
sub: The Lord's anointed • Kiss the Son
refs: Psalm 2:1

--- Why do the heathen rage?
kicker: Psalm 2 · The heathen rage
scripture: Psalm 2:1-2 | mark: Why do the heathen rage; against the LORD, and against his anointed
p: Why do the heathen rage? Why do people think they can go against God? Kings and rulers, presidents and prime ministers, popes and priests, all seek to be freed from the ways of the Lord, to do what they will.
p: Especially the religious and political leaders in the time of Jesus.
big: Acts 4:27

--- Let us break their bands asunder
kicker: Psalm 2 · The bands
big: Psalm 2:3
p: They believed if they killed the Son, they would break the bands, their bonds, and be free of God.
p: Jesus addressed this in a parable, about a Lord who planted a vineyard and lent it out to husbandmen to dress and keep it. And when the Lord sent servants to gather the harvest, the stewards beat and killed them. So finally, the Lord sends his son;

--- Come, let us kill him
kicker: Psalm 2 · The bands
big: Matthew 21:38 | mark: come, let us kill him
p: David prophetically writes of this here in **Psalm 2**.

--- He that sitteth in the heavens shall laugh
kicker: Psalm 2 · The Lord shall have them in derision
scripture: Psalm 2:4-5 | mark: the LORD shall have them in derision
p: But, it is folly. The Lord holds them in derision. He laughs at their vain efforts, because Christ, His anointed, rose again.

--- Thou art my Son
kicker: Psalm 2 · My holy hill of Zion
scripture: Psalm 2:6-7 | mark: Thou art my Son; this day have I begotten thee
p: He set His Son upon Zion, and upon the throne.
scripture: Acts 13:32-33 | mark: he hath raised up Jesus again

--- A rod of iron
kicker: Psalm 2 · The inheritance
scripture: Psalm 2:8-9 | mark: the heathen for thine inheritance; a rod of iron
p: Jesus' inheritance are all the nations of the world, and possession of the uttermost parts of the world. And as Lord of lords and King of kings, He will rule them.
scripture: Revelation 2:26-27 | mark: he shall rule them with a rod of iron

--- Be wise now therefore, O ye kings
kicker: Psalm 2 · A warning to the rulers
big: Psalm 2:10
p: This prophetic Psalm gives warning to the rulers of this world, reminding them of their place. That they serve at the pleasure of the Lord, and He will deliver vengeance.
p: It's an amazing and consistent message, across the entire Bible; The Christ is God's anointed, and Jesus is the Christ. All who believe on Christ Jesus shall be saved. His grace and mercy giving us the chance to serve Him in righteousness.

--- Kiss the Son
kicker: Psalm 2 · Kiss the Son
scripture: Psalm 2:11-12 | mark: Kiss the Son; Blessed are all they that put their trust in him
center: Amen

=== Psalm 3
--- Psalm 3 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 3
sub: The Lord my shield
refs: Psalm 3:1

--- When the world seems against you
kicker: Psalm 3 · Many rise up against me
scripture: Psalm 3:1-2 | mark: There is no help for him in God
p: When the world seems against you, and the wicked seem to prosper, and scoffers mock your trust in the Lord, we can grow weary and discouraged.
p: Knowing there are others who share this feeling, and have experienced similar despair, but found comfort and solace in prayer, as David does here, is such a blessing. And it is part of what makes the Bible so different from other religious texts.

--- The lifter up of mine head
kicker: Psalm 3 · Thou art a shield for me
scripture: Psalm 3:3-4 | mark: thou, O LORD, art a shield for me; the lifter up of mine head
p: The Lord is our shield. He does lift up our heads when we are feeling down. When everything feels lost, and we don't have the strength to continue we can cry out to the Lord, and He will hear us.

--- The Lord sustained me
kicker: Psalm 3 · I awaked
scripture: Psalm 3:5-6 | mark: I awaked; for the LORD sustained me
p: The same Lord over all that gives us life, and a new day and another chance, is the same Lord Who protects us from all evil.

--- Vengeance is His
kicker: Psalm 3 · Arise, O Lord
big: Psalm 3:7
p: Vengeance is His. And He is faithful to answer our plea for it. Turn to the Lord rather than wrath or despair, and see His faithful love for us.

--- Salvation belongeth unto the Lord
kicker: Psalm 3 · Thy blessing is upon thy people
p: Knowing that
big: Psalm 3:8 | mark: Salvation belongeth unto the LORD

=== Psalm 4
--- Psalm 4 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 4
sub: Prayer at night
refs: Psalm 4:1

--- Psalms of prayer
kicker: Psalm 4 · Prayer
p: Psalms of prayer are the most common. The cries to the Lord to save us from affliction and thanksgiving for having delivered us.
p: We often don't know how to pray, let alone in the midst of trouble. We often don't take our troubles directly to God. And when we do, we are at a loss for what to say.
big: Romans 8:26 | mark: we know not what we should pray for as we ought
p: But the Psalms are given as a foundation of prayer, to know we have a strong deliverer, a Lord that hears us, Who has given us help in our distress and Who grants mercy to His people.

--- O God of my righteousness
kicker: Psalm 4 · Hear me when I call
big: Psalm 4:1 | mark: O God of my righteousness
p: David calls out to the Lord, *O God of my righteousness,* not telling God of his own righteousness, but the knowledge that whatever righteousness we have, is His.
p: David acknowledges that the Lord has delivered him in the past, and is asking to be heard to do it again. Praise and gratitude. Humility. These are the foundations of an effective prayer.

--- They seek after leasing
kicker: Psalm 4 · The sons of men
big: Psalm 4:2 | mark: seek after leasing
p: Lamenting the ways of those who trouble us, the sons of men who vex the righteous with their filthy conversation. They scoff at the faithful. They love vain things, worldly ideas that profit nothing. They seek after leasing, which means to pursue lies, falsehoods, or deceit. But we are not like they are.
p: Or at least we shouldn't be.

--- Him that is godly for himself
kicker: Psalm 4 · Set apart
big: Psalm 4:3 | mark: the LORD hath set apart him that is godly for himself
p: The Lord has set us apart, His holy people. And He will hear when we reach out to Him.

--- Stand in awe, and sin not
kicker: Psalm 4 · Upon your bed
big: Psalm 4:4 | mark: commune with your own heart upon your bed, and be still
p: How often do we lay awake in prayer. Pondering our own sins, and seeking the Lord? How often do we wake in the morning, and praise God for His mercy, asking to be used by Him today?

--- The sacrifices of righteousness
kicker: Psalm 4 · Put your trust in the Lord
big: Psalm 4:5
p: We have the power given us by Christ, to be sons of God. To boldly go before the throne. Stand in awe, and sin not. Make yourselves a living sacrifice, in all you do. Put your trust in the Lord. Comfort yourself with this relationship with Him, that He has mercifully granted to us.

--- Lift thou up the light of thy countenance
kicker: Psalm 4 · Who will shew us any good?
big: Psalm 4:6 | mark: lift thou up the light of thy countenance upon us
p: David writes of the many who have no hope, who don't know the Lord is good to those that love Him. Don't despair as they do. Ask Him for His grace, to shine His light upon us. To look your way. Going to Him in confidence that whatever is His will, it shall be done. Those that trust in the Lord shall not be ashamed.

--- Gladness in my heart
kicker: Psalm 4 · Gladness
big: Psalm 4:7 | mark: Thou hast put gladness in my heart
p: Let His light fill you, rejoice. We are more blessed than those of the world. More blessed than those in time of bounty. He has made us His children. He has given us eternal life. Rejoice in that. Be still and know He is faithful.

--- I will both lay me down in peace
kicker: Psalm 4 · In peace, and sleep
big: Psalm 4:8 | mark: I will both lay me down in peace, and sleep
center: Amen!
p: And in the dark night, when you call out to Him in prayer, know He is with you, even His Spirit that dwells within you.

=== Psalm 5
--- Psalm 5 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 5
sub: Prayer in the morning
refs: Psalm 5:1

--- My King, and my God
kicker: Psalm 5 · Give ear to my words
scripture: Psalm 5:1-2 | mark: my King, and my God: for unto thee will I pray
p: The song of **Psalm 5** opens again with prayer, asking the Lord to hear and harken to the singer. David, the psalmist, King of Israel calls out to *his* King and his God.
p: Many of the Psalms open with this plea, and serve as a model for prayer. "Lord, my God, hear me" is an excellent start any time you go boldly before the Throne. *Unto Thee will I pray.*

--- In the morning
kicker: Psalm 5 · In the morning
big: Psalm 5:3 | mark: in the morning
p: And as **Psalm 4** was about praying in the night before sleeping, here in **Psalm 5** it is prayer to start the morning. Day and night, we should be in prayer to our Father. Directing our prayer to Him.
scripture: Matthew 6:9-10 | mark: Hallowed be thy name

--- Thou hatest all workers of iniquity
kicker: Psalm 5 · Hallowed be His name
scripture: Psalm 5:4-5 | mark: thou hatest all workers of iniquity
p: Hallowed be His name. He is holy, and hates the wicked, the foolish, the workers of iniquity.

--- In thy fear will I worship
kicker: Psalm 5 · Into thy house
scripture: Psalm 5:6-7 | mark: the bloody and deceitful man; in thy fear will I worship
p: We see imprecatory cursing of the liars and the bloody deceitful men, followed by the pledge to submit to God's mercy, and in fear worship Him in His house.

--- Make thy way straight
kicker: Psalm 5 · Lead me
big: Psalm 5:8 | mark: make thy way straight before my face
p: Then, the plea to show us His will, that the Lord's paths be made known to us, by His righteousness and in the face of our enemies seeking to destroy us, asking that God's will be done.

--- An open sepulchre
kicker: Psalm 5 · Their throat
big: Psalm 5:9 | mark: their throat is an open sepulchre
p: The enemies of God are wicked, and their throat, an open sepulchre, an open grave, giving off the stench of their own corruption.
p: Jesus uses this term so effectively in **Matthew 23** against the Pharisees.

--- Whited sepulchres
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 5:9
scripture: Matthew 23:27-28 | mark: whited sepulchres
p: He curses these workers of iniquity, in terms they know from scripture. The death their words bring forth to those that follow them and their lies, Jesus will say to them, begone, *I never knew you.*

--- Destroy thou them, O God
kicker: Psalm 5 · Cast them out
big: Psalm 5:10 | mark: let them fall by their own counsels
p: The Psalmist asks God to destroy these people that rise against the Lord and against truth. To bring them down by their own wicked plans, to cast them out in their rebellion against God.

--- Let them ever shout for joy
kicker: Psalm 5 · Rejoice
big: Psalm 5:11 | mark: let them ever shout for joy
p: But, for those that trust in the Lord, rejoice. Shout for joy! Let those that love His precious name, who love Jesus, forever shout for joy, because the Lord defends us from the wicked. And redeemed us from our sin in His mercy and grace.

--- As with a shield
kicker: Psalm 5 · Thou wilt bless the righteous
big: Psalm 5:12 | mark: with favour wilt thou compass him as with a shield
center: Amen.

